  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Eleven churches in Ethiopia were carved downward out of bedrock. Lalibela, up in the Amhara highlands. No blocks, no mortar, no scaffolding.",
        "visual_query": "ethiopian rock hewn church lalibela",
        "visual_fallback": "rock church"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Crews cut trenches straight down into volcanic rock, then hollowed out the block they had left standing in the middle. Roof first. Floor last.",
        "visual_query": "chisel carving volcanic rock wall",
        "visual_fallback": "carved rock"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Four of them stand completely free of the mountain. Tunnels run between the groups. One, Bete Giyorgis, is a giant cross sunk into the ground.",
        "visual_query": "ethiopian highland church stone trench",
        "visual_fallback": "stone trench"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Tradition gives all of it to one king, Gebre Meskel Lalibela, around 1200, and says the eleven took twenty-four years. The rock disagrees.",
        "visual_query": "ethiopian orthodox priest highlands",
        "visual_fallback": "highland priest"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Archaeologist David Phillipson dated the earliest cuttings to the seventh or eighth century. Four or five building phases, five hundred years of work, and one king's name on the whole thing.",
        "visual_query": "amhara highlands landscape ethiopia",
        "visual_fallback": "highland landscape"
      }
    ],
